WEBVTT

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:02.730
Coding agents can change your software

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.490
faster than anyone can read it.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.740
The problem is understanding.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.943
What your software actually does

00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:14.140
is scattered across code, tests, tickets, and old chats.

00:00:14.644 --> 00:00:16.794
So changes get planned on guesses,

00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:18.643
and approved on faith.

00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:20.899
Source Twin solves this

00:00:20.919 --> 00:00:23.493
with a plain-English twin of your codebase.

00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:25.563
Here's an example:

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:28.523
Paperlane, a small, made-up subscription app.

00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:31.173
Its twin is ordinary Markdown,

00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:33.633
in the same Git repository as its code.

00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:36.458
Each file explains one area of behavior

00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:38.311
in words anyone can read,

00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:40.071
and links it to the exact functions

00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:42.002
and tests behind it.

00:00:42.862 --> 00:00:45.232
Key ideas, like subscription and renewal,

00:00:45.252 --> 00:00:47.744
are defined once as shared terms,

00:00:47.764 --> 00:00:50.781
so people and agents mean the same thing everywhere.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:52.661
Now, a change.

00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:54.755
In Paperlane, cancelling an annual plan

00:00:54.775 --> 00:00:56.659
ends access right away,

00:00:56.679 --> 00:00:59.141
even though the customer paid for the whole year.

00:01:00.431 --> 00:01:01.348
You ask your coding agent

00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:04.771
to let annual customers keep access until renewal.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:08.020
Before touching code, the agent reads the twin,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.349
and follows a link to the renewal rule.

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:12.069
There's a catch.

00:01:12.399 --> 00:01:15.809
At renewal, every active subscription is charged.

00:01:15.829 --> 00:01:18.029
Those customers would pay again.

00:01:18.554 --> 00:01:20.084
You decide, in plain English:

00:01:20.254 --> 00:01:24.164
a scheduled cancellation ends at renewal, with no charge.

00:01:25.724 --> 00:01:27.824
The agent updates the twin first.

00:01:28.064 --> 00:01:31.111
This short English diff is what your team agrees on,

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:32.944
before any code changes.

00:01:33.588 --> 00:01:34.345
Then it follows the links

00:01:34.365 --> 00:01:36.698
to the exact code that has to change,

00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:38.714
and adds a test for each rule,

00:01:38.734 --> 00:01:40.548
including the catch it found.

00:01:41.638 --> 00:01:44.608
Now annual customers keep what they paid for.

00:01:46.024 --> 00:01:47.414
Source Twin check validates

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:49.794
every file, term, and link,

00:01:49.814 --> 00:01:50.914
and shows that the English

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:52.944
and the code changed together.

00:01:53.738 --> 00:01:56.588
Coverage shows what's connected, and what isn't.

00:01:57.428 --> 00:01:59.818
Then your team reviews the behavior, the code,

00:01:59.838 --> 00:02:02.388
and the tests together, in Git.

00:02:02.958 --> 00:02:05.618
Source Twin's checks prove the links are valid,

00:02:05.638 --> 00:02:07.308
not that the English is right.

00:02:07.648 --> 00:02:09.228
People still review the meaning.

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:11.454
It's open source,

00:02:11.555 --> 00:02:14.175
and it works with the coding agent you already use.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.503
Install it, run init, and ask your agent

00:02:17.523 --> 00:02:20.540
to propose the first areas for you to approve.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.480
So, to sum up:

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.520
Source Twin is a plain-English twin of your codebase.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.040
Written for people.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:28.770
Followed by agents.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.080
Reviewed in Git.
